“Interpretable reference mapping requires the incorporation of domain knowledge19, such as curated GPs, into the representation learning model to guide interpretation and exploration.” (Lotfollahi et al., 2022, p. 3)
\par
“single-cell representation learning” (Lotfollahi et al., 2022, p. 3)
\par
“We construct an “explainable programmable mapper” (expiMap) as an interpretable conditional variational autoencoder7,27,28 (CVAE) that allows the incorporation of domain knowledge by performing “architecture programming”, i.e., constraining the network architecture to ensure that each latent dimension captures the variability of known GPs.” (Lotfollahi et al., 2022, p. 3) OK, so we'e constraining things to ensure that the latent space has interpretability with respect to GPs
\par
“attention-like mechanism” (Lotfollahi et al., 2022, p. 3)
\par
“This knowledge is transformed into a binary GP matrix, in which each row is a gene program, and each column denotes the membership of a gene in that program” (Lotfollahi et al., 2022, p. 4)
\par
“architecture programming i.e. wiring the weights of the network using the GP matrix such that each latent variable contributes to the reconstruction of a set of genes defined by the GP.” (Lotfollahi et al., 2022, p. 4) So each latent variable is mapped from the GP, so it's influence on the predictions can be mapped directly back to the GP. Smart.
\par
“The adopted variational autoencoder architecture7,34 leverages a nonlinear encoder for flexibility and a linear decoder for interpretability.” (Lotfollahi et al., 2022, p. 4) This might be possible within scVI. It's not really relevant for us, since we essentially through the decoder away and instead us a ML supervised learning architecture.
\par
“he latent space dimension chosen is equal to the number of GPs, and the weights from each latent dimension (i.e., latent GP) to output are programmed according to the GP matrix so that a latent GP can only contribute to the reconstruction of genes in a particular GP” (Lotfollahi et al., 2022, p. 4)
\par
“we allow the inclusion of other genes in each GP by applying L1 sparsity regularization to genes not initially labeled to belong to that GP” (Lotfollahi et al., 2022, p. 4) So the L1 regularization means that we get a small number of genes.
\par
“To select only informative GPs, an attention-like mechanism is implemented with a group lasso regularization layer in latent space” (Lotfollahi et al., 2022, p. 4)
\par
“The model is trained in an end-to-end manner and can thus be used to construct reference atlases with interpretable embedding dimensions” (Lotfollahi et al., 2022, p. 4) What does "interpretable embedding dimensions" mean?
\par
“We modify the strategy of fine-tuning conditional weights in scArches allowing the model to learn new GPs that are not included in the reference model. This is achieved by the addition of new latent space dimensions, i.e., nodes with trainable weights in the bottleneck layer of the model (Fig. 1d, see methods), while keeping the rest frozen.” (Lotfollahi et al., 2022, p. 4)
\par
“We further employ the Hilbert–Schmidt Independence Criterion (HSIC)21,40, a kernel-based measure of latent variable independence40, to enforce independence between old and new unconstrained GPs learned during query optimization” (Lotfollahi et al., 2022, p. 5)
\par
(Lotfollahi et al., 2022, p. 5) There can be overlap in gene programs!!},
  file = {/Users/abhijit/Zotero/storage/QL6YJGFF/Lotfollahi et al. - 2022 - Biologically informed deep learning to infer gene program activity in single cells.pdf}
}

@article{lotfollahi:2022a,
  title = {Mapping Single-Cell Data to Reference Atlases by Transfer Learning},
